Revision history for Rex-GPU

{{$NEXT}}
  - gpu_detect recognises NVIDIA vGPU guest devices (Azure NVadsA10 v5, AWS
    G6f, ...) by PCI device and subsystem ID, read with a read-only
    `lspci -vmmnn -d 10de:` on hosts with an NVIDIA GPU: each NVIDIA GPU
    gets subsystem_vendor_id, subsystem_id, vgpu (0|1) and vgpu_type;
    compute is unchanged. New Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU holds NVIDIA's table
    (open-gpu-kernel-modules 615.71.09, MIT). install_driver / gpu_setup on
    a vGPU guest without a working driver die before changing the host,
    asking for the licensed vGPU guest driver, also next to a non-vGPU GPU;
    with a working driver they go on as usual. Not hardware-verified
    (karr #24)
  - install_driver and install_container_toolkit on openSUSE Leap wait up
    to 120 seconds for the zypp lock (ZYPP_LOCK_TIMEOUT, like apt's
    DPkg::Lock::Timeout) on every zypper call, so a fresh boot where
    cloud-init still holds it no longer fails at once with exit 7.
    Not hardware-verified (karr #53)
  - install_driver (and so gpu_setup) on an HGX host with NVSwitch whose
    driver is already installed, e.g. one provisioned before Fabric Manager
    support: if no Fabric Manager is installed and the host's own package
    sources offer it at exactly the loaded driver's version (apt-get update,
    then apt-cache madison plus a simulated install that removes nothing;
    dnf list --showduplicates), it is installed, verified with dpkg/rpm, and
    its service enabled and started. The driver and the package sources are
    never changed; not offered, it only warns with the reason and the
    version needed. An installed Fabric Manager of another version is left
    alone with a warning. Not hardware-verified (karr #50)
  - install_driver and install_container_toolkit on openSUSE Leap die when
    zypper cannot add or refresh NVIDIA's GFX / container toolkit
    repository (e.g. an HTTP error, an unresolvable host, the zypp lock),
    naming alias, URL and zypper's output, before any package is installed.
    A repository that fails its refresh is removed again instead of being
    left enabled. Re-runs still replace an existing entry. Not
    hardware-verified (karr #52)
  - install_driver on RHEL/Rocky/Alma/CentOS dies when dnf config-manager
    --add-repo cannot add NVIDIA's CUDA repository (e.g. an HTTP error on
    the .repo URL), naming the URL and dnf's output, before any driver
    package is installed. It used to carry on and fail later with
    "nvidia-driver not installed after dnf install". A re-run whose .repo
    is already in place but whose download fails now dies too.
    Not hardware-verified (karr #47)
  - gpu_detect (and so gpu_setup and Rex::Rancher gpu => 1) installs
    nothing when lspci is already on the host's PATH (command -v lspci).
    Without it, pciutils goes through Rex::Pkg as before, except on Rocky,
    AlmaLinux and CentOS Stream under their lsb_release names, where
    Rex::Pkg died "OS/Provider not supported": there it is installed with
    dnf and checked with rpm -q. Detection now dies if that check fails or
    lspci is still missing, instead of reporting no GPU.
    Not hardware-verified (karr #46)
  - gpu_setup installs NVIDIA Fabric Manager on HGX hosts with NVSwitch
    (HGX-2, A100, H100/H200), without which CUDA does not initialise there.
    gpu_detect returns a new nvswitch key (NVIDIA PCI class 0680 bridges
    with a known NVSwitch ID or name; [] elsewhere) and install_driver takes
    nvswitches => [...]. The Fabric Manager (Ubuntu nvidia-fabricmanager-NNN,
    NVIDIA's CUDA repo nvidia-fabricmanager) is installed after the driver
    at exactly its version, checked with dpkg/rpm, and its service enabled.
    Debian's non-free has none, so such a Debian 12/13 host gets NVIDIA's
    CUDA-repo driver; Debian 11 and openSUSE die before the host is changed.
    Hosts without NVSwitch are unchanged. Not hardware-verified (karr #23)
  - install_driver (and so gpu_setup) on an HGX B200/B300 (GPU device ID
    2901, 2909 or 3182; their NVSwitches are not on the host PCI bus, so no
    Fabric Manager is installed) installs the driver as before, then warns
    once, also for an already-installed driver, that CUDA needs NVIDIA
    Fabric Manager, the NVLink Subnet Manager (nvlsm), OFED/MOFED and
    kernel >= 5.17, none of which Rex::GPU installs, and whether
    nvidia-fabricmanager.service is active. GB200/GB300 (2941, 31C2, 31C3)
    get an info line that multi-node NVLink needs nvidia-imex. The IDs are
    Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Setup's overridable nvlink_platform_ids.
    Not hardware-verified (karr #49)
  - install_driver and install_container_toolkit accept Rocky Linux,
    AlmaLinux and CentOS Stream under the names Rex reports when
    lsb_release is installed (Rocky, RockyLinux, AlmaLinux, CentOSStream),
    which Rex's is_redhat does not know; they died "Unsupported OS" there.
    On such a host epel-release (and the RHEL 10 versionlock plugin) is
    installed with dnf and checked with rpm -q, since Rex::Pkg cannot run
    there. On RHEL itself (/etc/os-release ID=rhel, read by the
    RHEL setup) EPEL comes from its release RPM (epel-release-latest-N) and
    must be installed or the call dies before the driver, and CodeReady
    Builder is enabled with subscription-manager (a failure only warns);
    `pkg epel-release` had no candidate there. Rocky and Alma without
    lsb_release are unchanged. Not hardware-verified (karr #39)
  - Detection decides "compute" by GPU generation from the PCI device ID,
    not by marketing name: every Maxwell or newer NVIDIA GPU is compute
    (10de:1340 and up to Blackwell Ultra), GeForce MX, GT 1030, GTX 750 Ti
    and GTX 9xx included, whatever its PCI class and even when a stale
    pci.ids makes lspci print only "Device [10de:XXXX]". gpu_setup (also
    via Rex::Rancher gpu => 1) now installs a driver for them: the
    proprietary 580 branch for Maxwell/Pascal/Volta, as for the V100.
    Kepler and older (below 10de:1340, last branch 470) are compute => 0
    with a warning and skipped at any PCI class, so neither a Kepler
    display card (GT 710, GTX 780, Quadro K4000, GTX TITAN) nor a
    class-0302 Kepler Tesla (K80, K40, K20) stops the install for a newer
    GPU; a host with only such a card installs nothing and goes on.
    The verdict is the compute flag of Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Requirement's
    generation table (1, 0, or undef where no row covers the ID); name
    rules apply only to IDs outside the table, unknown stays 0.
    Not hardware-verified (karr #21, karr #45, karr #54, karr #55)
  - install_driver counts a driver as installed only if nvidia-smi -L lists
    a GPU and libcuda.so.1 is in the linker cache (ldconfig -p). A host with
    a working nvidia-smi but no CUDA library now gets a warning and the
    driver install instead of a skip. Debian's non-free driver installs and
    verifies libcuda1 explicitly (nvidia-driver pulls it only through
    recommends). Not hardware-verified (karr #42)
  - install_driver checks only the driver after an install (module,
    nvidia-smi, libcuda; new Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::verify_nvidia_driver, not
    exported), so it no longer warns "nvidia-container-toolkit not found"
    before the toolkit step. gpu_setup runs the full verify_nvidia as its
    last step instead, also when the driver was already installed;
    verify_nvidia itself is unchanged (karr #42)
  - install_driver(gpu => { device_id => '2b85', name => '...' }) needs no
    gpu_detect: only device_id and name are read, and install_driver never
    runs lspci or installs pciutils. A device_id that is not four hex digits
    (e.g. sysfs "0x2b85") now dies before the host is touched instead of
    counting as an unknown GPU. New example eg/ubuntu-drivers/: a custom
    setup that takes the Ubuntu package from ubuntu-drivers list --gpgpu
    (karr #42)
  - install_container_toolkit skips an installed toolkit (nvidia-ctk
    --version runs and dpkg/rpm lists nvidia-container-toolkit), e.g. on a
    re-run or a DGX OS image. A re-run therefore no longer upgrades the
    toolkit; do that with the package manager. On Debian/Ubuntu the apt
    timers are stopped first and curl/gnupg install via apt-get with the
    dpkg lock timeout instead of Rex::Pkg, so the dpkg lock held after the
    first-deploy reboot no longer fails the install. The repository key is
    replaced on every install (a rotated key is picked up), and a failed
    download or dearmor dies instead of being ignored. On RHEL/Rocky/Alma
    the .repo file is fetched with curl -f and put in place only if it has
    the [nvidia-container-toolkit] section, so an HTTP error page no longer
    lands in /etc/yum.repos.d: the call dies and an existing .repo is kept.
    openSUSE now checks the install with rpm -q and dies if the package is
    missing. Not hardware-verified (karr #37, #38, #42, #27, #44)
  - install_driver on openSUSE Leap verifies every driver install: the kmp
    meta package and the kernel module package it requires must be
    installed (rpm -q --whatprovides), or it dies with "... not installed
    after zypper install". The open G06/G07 driver used to be unchecked, so
    a failed zypper install was reported as success. openSUSE is unverified
    and unsupported; this can newly die there. Not hardware-verified
    (karr #27)
  - Ubuntu: the newest nvidia-driver-NNN-server(-open) package is looked up
    after apt-get update, not before, so a fresh image with stale or empty
    package lists no longer gets an old branch. If the refreshed index lists
    no such package, install_driver and gpu_setup die before installing a
    driver package; the silent nvidia-driver-570-server(-open) fallback is
    gone. Setup classes gain the step resolve_plan (after prepare_source),
    where resolve_source now runs; override resolve_source to choose the
    package another way. Not hardware-verified (karr #35)
  - Use your own driver setup class: gpu_setup / install_driver take
    setup => 'My::GPU::Setup' (or an object), and
    `set gpu_nvidia_setup => 'My::GPU::Setup'` in the Rexfile does the same
    for every call, Rex::Rancher's gpu => 1 included. Otherwise the class is
    still chosen by OS. The class is a subclass of a Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Setup
    class (experimental API), loaded from the project's lib/ or defined in
    the Rexfile. A missing or broken class dies before anything is done on
    the host. New requirement => { kernel_module, min_branch, max_branch }
    narrows the driver choice. It is intersected with what the GPUs need
    and can never override it; if no driver meets both, the call dies before
    the install. Example in eg/custom-setup/. Not hardware-verified
    (karr #34)
  - gpu_setup hands every CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU to install_driver (new
    option gpus => [...]; gpu => $one still works) and installs one driver
    that fits all of them: an Ada next to a B200 gets the open driver, an
    Ada next to a V100 the proprietary 580 one. GPUs that cannot share a
    driver (a V100 next to a B200) or a Kepler anywhere make install_driver
    and gpu_setup die before the host is changed, naming the GPUs.
    Rex::Rancher's gpu => 1 gets this without a change there. Each distro's
    Setup class tries an ordered list of driver sources and installs the
    first that fits; if none does it dies untouched, listing each source and
    why it was rejected (e.g. a V100 on a Debian release other than
    11/12/13, whose non-free branch is not known). Not hardware-verified
    (karr #33)
  - install_driver now runs through new Setup classes (experimental API:
    Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Setup; the apt layer ::Setup::Apt with ::Setup::Debian
    / ::Setup::Ubuntu, the rpm layer ::Setup::Rpm with ::Setup::RHEL /
    ::Setup::SUSE), one overridable method per step: already_installed,
    plan (host-read-only), prepare_host, prepare_source, install_packages,
    verify_packages, post_install. The commands sent to the host and the
    package choice are unchanged on every distro (karr #31, #32)
  - install_driver on Debian enables contrib/non-free/non-free-firmware per
    Debian archive entry (*.debian.org, Hetzner's /debian/ mirror, the cloud
    mirror+file list), adding only missing components, in sources.list and
    in deb822 sources.list.d/*.sources (Debian 13, cloud images). Hosts with
    the bookworm installer's "main non-free-firmware" or with only deb822
    sources used to get no non-free, so nvidia-driver had no candidate and
    the install died. sources.list is now rewritten via `file` instead of
    sed; third-party lines are left untouched. A mirror of your own
    (apt-cacher-ng, a company or national mirror, which the sed also
    edited) counts when its entry has signed-by= / Signed-By: naming only
    /usr/share/keyrings/debian-archive-* keyrings, or when a
    Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Setup::Debian subclass overrides the new
    is_debian_archive_uri (or is_debian_archive_keyring); otherwise it is
    left alone and the warning (no Debian archive entry recognised) names
    that override and eg/custom-setup. Not hardware-verified
    (karr #36, #40, #41)
  - New Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Requirement (experimental API): which kernel
    module (open/proprietary/either) and driver branch range a GPU needs,
    looked up by PCI device ID in a table a subclass can extend, with
    satisfied_by/why_not (exact branches and "newest, at least N" sources)
    and an intersect that dies on a multi-GPU conflict. install_driver
    chooses the driver with it. GB10 (DGX Spark) needs branch 580 or newer:
    NVIDIA lists it first in 580.119.02 (karr #30, #33)
  - install_driver keeps pre-Turing GPUs (Maxwell/Pascal/Volta: V100, P100,
    P40, M60, ...; 10de:1340-1DF6) on the proprietary 580 branch, their last
    one: Ubuntu nvidia-driver-580-server (dies if apt has no candidate),
    RHEL 8/9 module stream nvidia-driver:580-dkms, RHEL 10 dnf versionlock
    '*nvidia*580*', both with kmod-nvidia-latest-dkms, openSUSE Leap 15/16
    nvidia-driver-G06-kmp-meta. They used to get the newest driver or the
    open module, which installed fine but never bound. The installed branch
    and kmod are verified. Debian is unchanged. A Kepler-or-older GPU (e.g.
    Tesla K80; below 10de:1340) now makes install_driver and gpu_setup die
    before the host is changed. Its last driver is the end-of-life 470
    branch, so install it by hand; a working driver is still skipped. Not
    hardware-verified (karr #26)
  - NVIDIA.pm DESCRIPTION describes the per-generation driver selection; it
    still said -open was chosen on aarch64/GB10 only (karr #28)
  - install_driver on Debian installs a Blackwell GPU's driver (B200/GB200/
    B300, GeForce RTX 50xx, RTX PRO Blackwell, GB10) from NVIDIA's CUDA apt
    repository: cuda-keyring for debian12/debian13 (x86_64, or sbsa on
    arm64), then nvidia-driver-cuda + nvidia-kernel-open-dkms, with Debian
    non-free left alone. It used to install Debian's nvidia-driver (535 on
    bookworm, 550 on trixie). Those versions cannot drive Blackwell, so dpkg
    reported the driver installed but the module never bound. Both open-set
    packages must be ii afterwards. A leftover Debian nvidia-driver no longer
    passes that check. A Blackwell GPU on any other Debian release or
    architecture now dies before the host is touched. Every other GPU on
    Debian is unchanged. Package names and the dependency closure were
    checked against the repository indexes. The install has not run on
    hardware (karr #18)
  - generate_cdi_specs POD: the CDI spec captures the MIG layout at generation
    time; say how to regenerate it after MIG reconfiguration (karr #19)
  - Fix detect() hiding a real GPU behind a virtual display: a single virtio/
    QEMU(QXL)/VMware/VirtualBox display line anywhere in lspci output made the
    whole scan return empty, so a GPU passthrough (vfio-pci) host or cloud GPU
    VM — which shows an emulated console next to the real card — reported no
    NVIDIA GPU and gpu_setup silently did nothing. Virtual displays are now
    skipped per line and the real card is detected; output with only virtual
    displays still returns empty arrays. NVIDIA vGPU guest devices (vendor
    10de) are indistinguishable from passthrough in lspci -nn and are now
    detected the same way. Not hardware-verified (karr #17)
  - configure_containerd (rke2/k3s) now heals an EARLIER containerd clobber: a
    host set up by the 0.001 release carries a stale full-config
    config.toml.tmpl (the bare imports=... + version=2 template, missing the
    Go-template include of the RKE2/K3s base config) that replaced it. RKE2/K3s
    render that stale template, so the generated config.toml already shows an
    nvidia runtime and the "already-wired" no-op that karr #9 added left the
    clobber — missing SystemdCgroup / pinned sandbox image / certs.d — in place.
    configure_containerd now detects that exact bare-clobber signature and
    removes the template before trusting the generated config, then writes the
    additive v3 drop-in; the distribution regenerates its native config on the
    next restart. The match is deliberately narrow (a new pure, offline-testable
    predicate _is_rke2_clobber_tmpl, new t/70-containerd-heal.t): it never
    removes the base-extending config.toml.tmpl, a user's own custom tmpl, or
    config-v3.toml.d/. It does NOT restart rke2/k3s (that would bounce the
    node's containerd and workloads) — it warns that the operator must restart
    the service or reboot for the native config to regenerate; config.toml is
    not self-healing. NB: not hardware-verified — no clobbered node was
    available and the containerd step never ran live (the pipeline died at the
    driver step, karr #7). A maintainer must reproduce a node clobbered by old
    0.002 against modern RKE2 (containerd 2.x / config v3), run configure_containerd,
    restart RKE2, and confirm the regenerated config.toml carries SystemdCgroup,
    the pinned sandbox image and the certs.d config_path plus the nvidia runtime
    (karr #13)
  - generate_cdi_specs no longer writes a static /etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml when a
    managed CDI source already owns the runtime scan dir. Modern
    nvidia-container-toolkit ships nvidia-cdi-refresh.path/.service, which keep
    /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml fresh across driver updates; /etc/cdi and /run/cdi are
    both default CDI scan dirs, so the old always-write behaviour defined
    nvidia.com/gpu twice when the managed source was present (risking a
    duplicate-device load error in CDI consumers) and the static copy drifted
    against the refreshed one. When the nvidia-cdi-refresh unit is installed or
    armed (or a /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml already exists), the managed generator is
    triggered once and left to own CDI; otherwise /etc/cdi is written as before.
    The decision is the pure, offline-testable predicate
    _cdi_managed_source_present (new t/60-cdi-managed-source.t). NB: not
    hardware-verified — the toolkit/CDI step never ran live (the pipeline died
    at the driver step, karr #7); a maintainer must confirm on a real node with
    nvidia-cdi-refresh installed that /etc/cdi stays empty, /run/cdi/nvidia.yaml
    is fresh, and the CDI consumer loads nvidia.com/gpu exactly once (karr #11)
  - aarch64 / NVIDIA DGX Spark (GB10) support, part 1 (detection + RHEL repo)
    and part 2 (Ubuntu open-kernel-module driver selection). Detection
    classifies the GB10 (PCI 10de:2e12) as compute by its device ID (the
    Blackwell range, see the karr #45 entry): it enumerates as a VGA
    controller [0300] and, on a host whose pci.ids predates the silicon,
    lspci prints only "Device" with no marketing name, so the name-token
    rules classified it compute => 0 and gpu_setup skipped the whole
    pipeline on a Spark (verified live on cortex). The unknown-model
    default is unchanged (still 0). The RHEL/Rocky/Alma CUDA repository URL is
    architecture-aware: aarch64 uses the "sbsa" tree (repos/rhelN/sbsa/),
    x86_64 is unchanged — the old hardcoded x86_64 pointed aarch64 at a
    nonexistent repo. gpu_setup now threads the detected GPU (name + PCI
    device ID) through to install_driver's new optional gpu option; on
    Ubuntu, on x86_64 and aarch64 alike, a Blackwell-architecture GPU
    (B200/GB200/B300, GeForce RTX 50xx, RTX PRO Blackwell, GB10 — none has a
    proprietary kernel module) now gets the -server-open package variant
    instead of the default -server one, which the previous auto-detect
    explicitly filtered *-open* candidates OUT of; before, an RTX 50xx or
    B200 on x86_64 got a -server driver dpkg reports installed but whose
    module never binds. Blackwell is recognised by PCI device ID (0x2900-
    0x2FFF plus B300/GB300 3182, 31C2-31C3, per NVIDIA's open-gpu-kernel-
    modules supported-GPU table); every other GPU, including an unknown
    future ID, picks -server exactly as before (karr #16). New pure,
    offline-testable predicates
    Rex::GPU::Detect::open_kernel_module_required and
    Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::_ubuntu_needs_open_kernel_module drive the decision
    (new t/80-ubuntu-open-driver.t); the -server-open package name was
    confirmed published for arm64 against Ubuntu's Launchpad archive, not
    against a live apt-cache. NB: NEITHER the RHEL aarch64 install NOR this
    Ubuntu -open install has run on real hardware — cortex (the only GB10
    host) already has its driver loaded (DGX-OS prebuilt), so the karr #10
    idempotency short-circuit skips install_driver there entirely, and no
    fresh Ubuntu/RHEL aarch64 Spark was available, nor any x86_64 Blackwell
    host; a maintainer must confirm both against one (karr #14/#15/#16)
  - Make install_driver idempotent against a host that already has a working
    NVIDIA driver (e.g. provisioned via the NVIDIA CUDA apt/dnf repo, or a
    re-run of gpu_setup): a distro-neutral short-circuit now skips the driver
    install when nvidia-smi -L already lists a GPU, before the per-distro
    package selection. Previously the Ubuntu branch auto-selected a versioned
    nvidia-driver-NNN-server whose libs Conflict with the installed
    (unversioned) userspace, so apt refused and gpu_setup died at the
    install-verify seam; the auto-detected version could also be LOWER than the
    installed one (a downgrade). The nouveau blacklist and reboot are skipped
    too, so the call is a clean no-op on an already-provisioned host (karr #10)
  - Fix containerd config for RKE2/K3s clobbering the distribution's native
    config on modern hosts. The old code wrote a full config.toml.tmpl
    (version=2, io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri) that REPLACED the RKE2/K3s-generated
    config — on containerd 2.x / config v3 that dropped SystemdCgroup, the
    pinned sandbox image, snapshotter opts and the certs.d config_path.
    configure_containerd is now version-aware and additive: a no-op when
    RKE2/K3s already wired the nvidia runtime natively, a config-v3.toml.d/
    drop-in on modern hosts, or a base-extending config.toml.tmpl (renders the
    distribution base first via its "base" template, then adds nvidia) on
    legacy config-v2 hosts. Handles RKE2 and K3s under their respective
    /var/lib/rancher/*/agent/etc/containerd/ dirs (karr #9)
  - Fix openSUSE Leap NVIDIA driver: operating_system_version() strips dots
    ("15.6" -> "156"), so int() routed *every* Leap through the >= 16 branch
    and Leap 15.x got the leap/16.0/ repo + G07 meta package instead of
    leap/15.x/ + G06. Derive the major from the raw operating_system_release()
    string; add offline unit test t/20-suse-driver.t (karr #6)
  - Document actual detected NVIDIA name form in the SYNOPSIS examples
    ("AD104GL [RTX 4000 SFF Ada Generation]", codename + bracketed marketing
    string) — the previous "NVIDIA RTX ..." example did not match the code (karr #5)
  - Rex::LibSSH >= 0.004 host-key verification: the eg/ deploy examples now
    KEEP verification on and ssh-keyscan the fresh host into known_hosts via a
    'before ALL' pre-connect hook (rancher_scan_known_hosts), consistent with
    Rex::Rancher and this dist's own POD recommendation. Override by adding
    disable_strict_host_key_checking to the feature list.
  - Mark openSUSE Leap / SLES as unverified and unsupported (POD + README):
    SUSE is not a deploy target for the GPU-on-Rancher pipeline; the verified
    set is the RKE2 Linux family (Debian/Ubuntu/RHEL/Rocky/Alma). The
    _install_driver_suse code stays but is no longer advertised as supported,
    consistent with Rex::Rancher's node-prep matrix.

0.001     2026-03-29 04:20:30Z
  - Initial release
  - GPU hardware detection via PCI class codes (NVIDIA, AMD)
  - NVIDIA driver installation (Debian 12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04, RHEL/Rocky 8/9/10, openSUSE Leap 15.6/16.0)
  - NVIDIA Container Toolkit installation with automatic repo setup
  - CDI spec generation via nvidia-ctk cdi generate
  - Containerd runtime configuration (RKE2, K3s, standalone)
  - Support for Ubuntu 24.04: auto-detect nvidia-driver-NNN-server, handle virtual nvidia-smi package
  - DPkg::Lock::Timeout=120 on all apt-get calls for resilience on fresh-boot systems
  - All package installs use apt-get/dnf directly with dpkg/rpm verification
    (Rex::Pkg dies on non-zero from DKMS/grub/initramfs post-install scripts)
  - Reboot support with reconnect polling via Rex::LibSSH
  - Tested on Hetzner dedicated servers with NVIDIA RTX 4000 SFF Ada Generation
    (Debian 13, Rocky Linux 10.1, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS)
  - eg/Rexfile example for GPU detection and cluster deployment
  - Requires Rex::LibSSH for deployment to SFTP-less hosts

